    "confidence": 0.8,
    "reasoning": "That a microscopic black hole produced at a collider would evaporate essentially instantly is the standard theoretical expectation of high-energy physics, and the evidence for it is strong but entirely theoretical: no microscopic black hole has ever been produced or observed, and Hawking radiation itself has never been directly detected. Within semiclassical gravity the conclusion is robust and multiply confirmed. The decay time of a black hole with a mass near a TeV-scale fundamental Planck scale comes out at roughly 10^-26 seconds, some twenty orders of magnitude below detector, trapping, and accretion timescales, whether computed from the canonical Planckian spectrum, integrated from the Stefan-Boltzmann law, or implemented in the event generators used to design LHC searches (https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/hep-ph/0307305, https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.0657).\n\nThe expectation rests on two premises. The first, [[claim:e9cafa78-effa-42ec-9cdb-d1c7fa57c46d|that the semiclassical calculation applies at collider-scale masses]], holds comfortably for holes several times heavier than the fundamental scale but loses quantitative control near the production threshold, where the entropy is small and quantum-gravity corrections are no longer negligible (https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0106219, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1104.5129). The second, [[claim:599f3b8d-2bef-4d83-bb4e-b2c082d070fd|that evaporation runs to completion rather than ending at a stable remnant]], is the standard result of Hawking theory but remains a genuinely open endpoint question, since no accepted theory describes the final Planck-scale stage; even papers that entertain stable remnants affirm that the semiclassical phase decays promptly (https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.3208, https://arxiv.org/html/2404.10070).\n\nThe credible disagreement is confined and has been answered in the literature. A 2008 analysis by Rainer Plaga (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1415), building on the microcanonical treatment of Casadio, Harms and Leblanc, argued that near-threshold holes could radiate so weakly as to persist, so that prompt evaporation cannot simply be assumed. Giddings and Mangano replied (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.4087) that the scenario applies the microcanonical formulas outside their range of validity, with radiation rates differing from the claimed values by large factors, and the later safety literature treats the objection as answered. The institutional position, stated in the CERN Scientific Policy Committee's review of the LHC Safety Assessment Group report, affirms the decay expectation while noting that the safety case was deliberately built not to depend on it: the astrophysical exclusion argument in Giddings and Mangano's own paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3381) holds even if Hawking radiation fails entirely. What would change this assessment is a completed account of the evaporation endpoint that predicts stability or strongly suppressed radiation, or empirical evidence bearing on Hawking evaporation itself, for instance from analogue systems or from the observation of microscopic black hole production."
  }
